1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,180
A Medicare Advantage plan can cost you $0 a month. And in one bad year,

2
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:09,120
it can take $12,250 out of your savings.

3
00:00:10,680 --> 00:00:13,160
That's not a scam, and it's not a glitch.

4
00:00:13,880 --> 00:00:18,080
It's written on page one of the plan, in a line most people never read.

5
00:00:18,780 --> 00:00:22,800
And for someone living off what they saved, it's the line that decides how

6
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:24,080
long the money lasts.

7
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:29,840
Medicare's open enrollment runs from October 15th to December 7th.

8
00:00:30,160 --> 00:00:34,200
Millions of people will pick a plan in the next eight weeks, mostly by looking

9
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:34,800
at the premium.

10
00:00:35,560 --> 00:00:38,160
So today, we read the fine print instead.

11
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:42,700
Three lines. The price, the cap, and the door.

12
00:00:43,560 --> 00:00:48,320
By the end, you'll know what one bad year costs under each choice, what

13
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:52,540
it does to the age your savings run out, and the one deadline that can't

14
00:00:52,540 --> 00:00:57,880
be undone. Every figure here comes from Medicare's own documents for 2027,

15
00:00:58,120 --> 00:00:59,600
or we'll name where it's from.

16
00:01:00,220 --> 00:01:04,180
It's education, not advice, and your plan's numbers will be its own.

17
00:01:05,140 --> 00:01:06,460
First, some quick ground.

18
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:09,960
At 65, you choose how to take Medicare.

19
00:01:10,700 --> 00:01:15,360
One way is Original Medicare, the government plan, usually with two add-ons,

20
00:01:15,780 --> 00:01:20,120
a drug plan and a supplement called Medigap that pays most of what Medicare

21
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:25,600
doesn't. The other way is Medicare Advantage, one private plan that replaces

22
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:30,160
all of it, often with drugs included, often with dental and vision thrown

23
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:34,060
in. Both cover the same doctors' visits and hospital stays.

24
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:37,360
What's different is who you pay and when.

25
00:01:38,820 --> 00:01:40,320
Line one, the price.

26
00:01:41,380 --> 00:01:46,180
The average Medicare Advantage premium for 2027 is about $12 a month.

27
00:01:46,840 --> 00:01:47,960
Many plans are zero.

28
00:01:48,820 --> 00:01:53,020
Medicare's own figures say eight in ten people in these plans can keep theirs

29
00:01:53,020 --> 00:01:55,400
next year at the same premium or lower.

30
00:01:56,420 --> 00:01:58,540
A Medigap plan costs real money.

31
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:05,400
The most popular one, called Plan G, averaged about $164 a month in 2023,

32
00:02:05,820 --> 00:02:09,280
the latest national figure, from the health policy group KFF.

33
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:15,360
Add a drug plan, about $36 a month on average next year, and the Part B

34
00:02:15,360 --> 00:02:17,360
deductible, $283.

35
00:02:18,580 --> 00:02:21,400
Let's put that side by side for a quiet year.

36
00:02:22,020 --> 00:02:23,880
No hospital. A few checkups.

37
00:02:24,360 --> 00:02:25,060
Some generics.

38
00:02:26,060 --> 00:02:30,760
Medigap, about $2,700 for the year, mostly premiums.

39
00:02:31,740 --> 00:02:36,600
Medicare Advantage, let's say about a thousand dollars in copays, and quiet

40
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:42,600
years add up. If you're healthy from 66 to 79 and costs rise about 5% a

41
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:47,160
year, Medicare Advantage leaves you roughly $33,000 ahead.

42
00:02:47,880 --> 00:02:52,040
That's real money. For someone who stays healthy, Advantage can be the better

43
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:55,580
deal, and anyone who tells you otherwise isn't doing the math.

44
00:02:56,180 --> 00:02:58,140
It also asks for something in return.

45
00:02:58,840 --> 00:03:00,020
Most plans have a network,

46
00:03:00,920 --> 00:03:03,140
doctors and hospitals they've signed contracts with.

47
00:03:03,560 --> 00:03:07,820
Go outside it on most plans and you pay the whole bill yourself, except

48
00:03:07,820 --> 00:03:12,180
in an emergency. And many treatments need the plan's permission first, called

49
00:03:12,180 --> 00:03:14,880
prior authorization, before it agrees to pay.

50
00:03:14,880 --> 00:03:18,620
None of that is hidden. It's how these plans keep the premium low.

51
00:03:19,180 --> 00:03:20,880
For a lot of people, it works fine.

52
00:03:21,620 --> 00:03:22,740
Medigap works differently.

53
00:03:23,380 --> 00:03:26,120
Any doctor in the country who takes Medicare takes you.

54
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:28,620
No network, no permission slips.

55
00:03:29,140 --> 00:03:32,280
You pay for that freedom every month, sick or well.

56
00:03:32,840 --> 00:03:38,160
How to check yours. On Medicare's Plan Finder at medicare.gov, put in your

57
00:03:38,160 --> 00:03:39,940
zip code and your actual prescriptions.

58
00:03:40,580 --> 00:03:44,920
It shows each plan's premium and its estimated yearly drug cost, side by

59
00:03:44,920 --> 00:03:48,520
side. But the premium is only the cost of a quiet year.

60
00:03:50,360 --> 00:03:51,800
Line two, the cap.

61
00:03:52,640 --> 00:03:56,520
Every Medicare Advantage plan has to set a ceiling on what you pay in a

62
00:03:56,520 --> 00:04:00,000
year for covered care. It's called the maximum out of pocket.

63
00:04:00,620 --> 00:04:03,520
Once you hit it, the plan pays the rest until January.

64
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:09,000
For 2027, the highest a plan is allowed to set it for care inside its network

65
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:11,080
is $9,850.

66
00:04:12,020 --> 00:04:13,900
That's up $600 from this year.

67
00:04:14,460 --> 00:04:18,240
If the plan lets you go outside its network, the combined ceiling can reach

68
00:04:18,240 --> 00:04:19,660
$14,800.

69
00:04:20,400 --> 00:04:22,120
Now, here's the part people miss.

70
00:04:22,640 --> 00:04:24,860
That ceiling doesn't include your prescriptions.

71
00:04:25,720 --> 00:04:27,800
Drugs have their own cap set by law.

72
00:04:28,340 --> 00:04:31,140
For 2027, it's $2,400.

73
00:04:31,960 --> 00:04:38,220
So, a bad year, a surgery, a cancer diagnosis, a few weeks in a rehab facility,

74
00:04:38,720 --> 00:04:43,320
can cost up to $9,850 plus $2,400.

75
00:04:43,820 --> 00:04:50,900
$12,250 in one year. And the meter resets every January 1st.

76
00:04:51,240 --> 00:04:52,780
Think about what that means for timing.

77
00:04:53,420 --> 00:04:56,520
Get sick in November and you can hit this year's cap by Christmas.

78
00:04:57,220 --> 00:05:01,400
On January 1st, the meter goes back to zero and the same treatment starts

79
00:05:01,400 --> 00:05:05,120
filling next year's cap. Two caps in four months.

80
00:05:05,620 --> 00:05:09,860
$24,500 out of savings in one winter.

81
00:05:10,420 --> 00:05:11,880
And the ceiling doesn't stay put.

82
00:05:12,360 --> 00:05:15,580
It's going up six and a half percent for 2027 alone.

83
00:05:16,140 --> 00:05:17,760
It can rise again every year.

84
00:05:18,080 --> 00:05:22,080
So, the bad year at 80 will likely cost more than the bad year at 66.

85
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:27,900
Now the same bad year with Medigap Plan G, the premiums, the drug plan,

86
00:05:28,280 --> 00:05:30,840
the Part B deductible, and that same drug cap.

87
00:05:31,420 --> 00:05:35,820
About $5,100. And it isn't only premiums you're comparing.

88
00:05:36,380 --> 00:05:41,120
Under Plan G, after the small Part B deductible, the supplement pays essentially

89
00:05:41,120 --> 00:05:43,440
all of your share of Medicare covered care.

90
00:05:44,360 --> 00:05:47,980
Hospital stays, surgery, the 20% Medicare doesn't cover.

91
00:05:48,460 --> 00:05:51,560
There's no cap to hit because there's almost nothing left to pay.

92
00:05:52,160 --> 00:05:54,400
So, that's the trade in plain numbers.

93
00:05:54,400 --> 00:05:58,160
Medicare Advantage has the lower floor and the higher ceiling.

94
00:05:58,860 --> 00:06:01,340
Medigap has the higher floor and the lower ceiling.

95
00:06:01,880 --> 00:06:05,280
And a ceiling matters most at the age you're most likely to hit it.

96
00:06:05,660 --> 00:06:09,180
How to check yours. Every plan sends a Summary of Benefits.

97
00:06:09,780 --> 00:06:12,620
The maximum out-of-pocket is near the top of page one.

98
00:06:13,120 --> 00:06:17,400
Find the in-network number and if it's a PPO, the combined number too.

99
00:06:17,960 --> 00:06:19,300
Then ask yourself one question.

100
00:06:20,100 --> 00:06:23,140
Could I pay that out of savings two years in a row?

101
00:06:24,640 --> 00:06:26,080
Let's run it on one example.

102
00:06:27,080 --> 00:06:28,140
Single. 66.

103
00:06:29,120 --> 00:06:31,500
$350,000 saved.

104
00:06:32,320 --> 00:06:35,820
Social Security covers the basics and savings make up the rest.

105
00:06:36,360 --> 00:06:42,040
$14,000 a year to start, that's 4%, rising with inflation at 3%.

106
00:06:42,040 --> 00:06:44,540
We'll assume 5% a year on the savings.

107
00:06:45,120 --> 00:06:47,080
Health costs rise 5% a year.

108
00:06:47,520 --> 00:06:50,420
Then we'll run it again at a more cautious 4%.

109
00:06:50,420 --> 00:06:54,940
One more thing first. With no health costs at all, this money would last

110
00:06:54,940 --> 00:06:59,620
past a hundred. Health care alone, before any choice we make, pulls that

111
00:06:59,620 --> 00:07:02,620
in by years. The only question is how many.

112
00:07:03,200 --> 00:07:04,680
First, the quiet life.

113
00:07:05,540 --> 00:07:06,900
Healthy every single year.

114
00:07:07,580 --> 00:07:10,220
Medigap. The money runs dry at 92.

115
00:07:10,920 --> 00:07:14,020
Medicare Advantage with its low premiums, 97.

116
00:07:14,840 --> 00:07:19,120
Five extra years. That's the case for Advantage and it's a strong one.

117
00:07:19,660 --> 00:07:21,640
Now the life most of us should plan for.

118
00:07:22,020 --> 00:07:24,720
Quiet until 80. Then a bad year.

119
00:07:25,260 --> 00:07:27,860
And another. And another. A heart.

120
00:07:28,280 --> 00:07:30,840
A hip. A diagnosis that doesn't go away.

121
00:07:31,560 --> 00:07:34,120
Medigap. The money runs dry at 90.

122
00:07:34,620 --> 00:07:36,440
Medicare Advantage, 88.

123
00:07:39,020 --> 00:07:42,740
At the cautious 4%, it's 87 against 86.

124
00:07:43,400 --> 00:07:46,300
The gap narrows. The direction doesn't change.

125
00:07:46,940 --> 00:07:48,300
Here's why it moves so fast.

126
00:07:48,900 --> 00:07:53,880
At 80, with inflation, this person is pulling about $21,000 a year from

127
00:07:53,880 --> 00:07:59,120
savings just to live. A bad year under Advantage, with costs rising, adds

128
00:07:59,120 --> 00:08:03,140
about 24,000 more on top. More than double the withdrawal.

129
00:08:03,720 --> 00:08:05,980
In one year. And again the next.

130
00:08:06,500 --> 00:08:08,040
Savings can handle one of those.

131
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:11,380
A string of them is what drains a nest egg years early.

132
00:08:11,840 --> 00:08:16,340
And look at what flipped. In the quiet life, Advantage won by five years.

133
00:08:16,840 --> 00:08:18,880
In the hard one, it lost by two.

134
00:08:19,360 --> 00:08:21,260
Same person, same savings.

135
00:08:21,740 --> 00:08:23,660
The only thing that changed was their health.

136
00:08:24,280 --> 00:08:28,140
How to check yours. Take your savings and what you pull out each year.

137
00:08:28,580 --> 00:08:34,260
Then add your plan's cap plus the drug cap every year from 78 to 82 and run

138
00:08:34,260 --> 00:08:37,950
it forward. If you've never run your own number, start with the one from

139
00:08:37,950 --> 00:08:40,870
episode one. Then put this on top of it.

140
00:08:41,290 --> 00:08:42,750
Which leaves the obvious question.

141
00:08:43,210 --> 00:08:46,630
If the hard years come, why not just switch to Medigap at 80?

142
00:08:47,190 --> 00:08:48,110
That's line three.

143
00:08:49,890 --> 00:08:51,670
Line three. The door.

144
00:08:52,710 --> 00:08:57,410
When you first sign up for Part B at 65 or older, you get a six-month window

145
00:08:57,410 --> 00:09:00,230
to buy any Medigap policy sold in your state.

146
00:09:00,810 --> 00:09:04,930
During those six months, the insurer has to sell it to you at its standard

147
00:09:04,930 --> 00:09:06,810
price, no matter your health.

148
00:09:07,390 --> 00:09:10,590
After that window closes, there's no federal guarantee.

149
00:09:11,270 --> 00:09:15,150
In most states, an insurer can ask about your health and charge you more

150
00:09:15,150 --> 00:09:16,430
or turn you down.

151
00:09:17,130 --> 00:09:21,870
There's one safety valve. If Medicare Advantage is your first plan at 65

152
00:09:21,870 --> 00:09:26,110
or the first time you've ever tried one and you leave within 12 months,

153
00:09:26,650 --> 00:09:29,070
federal law gives you a second chance at Medigap.

154
00:09:29,590 --> 00:09:31,510
After that first year, it's gone.

155
00:09:32,190 --> 00:09:34,290
And here's the trap inside the trap.

156
00:09:34,830 --> 00:09:39,350
Every fall and again from January to March, Medicare itself will let you

157
00:09:39,350 --> 00:09:41,810
leave Advantage and go back to Original Medicare.

158
00:09:42,430 --> 00:09:44,210
That part is easy, one form.

159
00:09:44,710 --> 00:09:48,690
But the Medigap supplement, the piece that puts a ceiling back on your costs,

160
00:09:48,970 --> 00:09:50,470
comes from a private insurer.

161
00:09:51,470 --> 00:09:54,970
Medicare letting you switch doesn't mean the insurer has to sell you a policy.

162
00:09:55,710 --> 00:09:57,170
A handful of states go further.

163
00:09:58,090 --> 00:10:02,490
Connecticut, Massachusetts, Maine, and New York let people 65 and over buy

164
00:10:02,490 --> 00:10:05,890
a Medigap plan later without being turned down for their health.

165
00:10:06,430 --> 00:10:09,350
Most states don't. So picture 80 again.

166
00:10:09,790 --> 00:10:11,090
The bad years have started.

167
00:10:11,530 --> 00:10:13,030
You'd like the lower ceiling now.

168
00:10:13,470 --> 00:10:15,450
And the insurer asks about your health.

169
00:10:15,790 --> 00:10:18,770
At exactly the moment, the answer is no longer good.

170
00:10:19,270 --> 00:10:21,330
That's the fine print that matters most.

171
00:10:21,770 --> 00:10:23,670
Not the premium, not even the cap.

172
00:10:24,170 --> 00:10:29,610
The door, which is wide open at 65 and in most states, quietly closes.

173
00:10:30,490 --> 00:10:31,270
How to check yours.

174
00:10:32,110 --> 00:10:34,470
Call your State Health Insurance Assistance Program.

175
00:10:34,850 --> 00:10:39,370
Every state has one. It's free, it's independent, and it's not selling anything.

176
00:10:39,950 --> 00:10:44,830
Ask one question. In my state, can I buy a Medigap plan later if my health

177
00:10:44,830 --> 00:10:45,170
changes?

178
00:10:46,570 --> 00:10:47,930
So three lines.

179
00:10:48,670 --> 00:10:50,390
The price is lower with Advantage.

180
00:10:50,870 --> 00:10:52,610
And in quiet years, that's real money.

181
00:10:53,110 --> 00:10:59,230
The cap is higher, up to $12,250 in a single bad year for 2027.

182
00:10:59,650 --> 00:11:01,270
And it comes back every January.

183
00:11:01,810 --> 00:11:04,310
And the door to switching is widest at 65.

184
00:11:04,890 --> 00:11:07,050
And in most states, it narrows fast.

185
00:11:07,790 --> 00:11:09,510
None of this makes one choice right.

186
00:11:09,950 --> 00:11:14,090
It makes it a bet on your health, placed at the age you know the least about

187
00:11:14,090 --> 00:11:16,430
it. One thing to do this week.

188
00:11:16,910 --> 00:11:21,250
Before December 7th, pull your plan's Summary of Benefits and write down the

189
00:11:21,250 --> 00:11:26,890
maximum out-of-pocket. Then ask, if that came out of my savings at 80 and

190
00:11:26,890 --> 00:11:27,990
again at 81,

191
00:11:28,770 --> 00:11:30,270
where does my money run out?

192
00:11:30,790 --> 00:11:34,050
Because that's the real question under every Medicare choice.

193
00:11:34,510 --> 00:11:36,630
Not which plan has the nicer extras.

194
00:11:37,270 --> 00:11:41,830
Whether the money you spent 40 years saving is still there at 88 when you

195
00:11:41,830 --> 00:11:46,170
can't go back to work and you still need to eat and heat the house and keep

196
00:11:46,170 --> 00:11:46,770
your medicine.

197
00:11:47,490 --> 00:11:50,450
A fee-only fiduciary can run that number with you.

198
00:11:50,950 --> 00:11:53,110
So can a free counselor from your state's program.

199
00:11:53,830 --> 00:11:55,410
If this was useful, subscribe.

200
00:11:56,030 --> 00:11:57,970
New numbers every Tuesday and Friday.

201
00:11:59,010 --> 00:12:01,850
Friday, the 2027 Social Security raise.

202
00:12:02,510 --> 00:12:05,570
And how much of it Medicare takes back before it reaches you.

203
00:12:06,170 --> 00:12:08,090
Will your money last as long as you do?

204
00:12:10,130 --> 00:12:12,550
Run your own number. See you Friday.

